{"availability":"available","full":"mizdra/eslint-interactive","name":"eslint-interactive","description":"The CLI tool to fix huge number of ESLint errors","category":"Developer tools","language":"TypeScript","stars":463,"forks":12,"created":"2020-12-19T10:08:31Z","updated":"2026-05-16T07:16:21Z","license":"MIT","branch":"main","homepage":"","topics":["eslint","javascript"],"demo":"https://stackblitz.com/edit/eslint-interactive-playground?file=src%2Findex.ts&view=editor","color":"#323057","ink":"#d6caff","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-11T09:01:45.279Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-11T09:01:45.279Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-11T09:01:45.279Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-11T09:01:45.279Z","stars":463,"forks":12}],"technologies":[],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["The default ESLint output contains a lot of useful messages for developers, such as the source of the error and hints for fixing it. While this works for many use cases, it does not work well in situations where many messages are reported. For example, when introducing ESLint into a project, or when making big changes to the eslint.config.js of a project. In these situations, the output of ESLint can be quite large, making it difficult for developers to analyze the output. It is also difficult for the developer to fix messages mechanically, because messages of many rules are mixed up in the…","In such the above situation, I think two things are important:","So, I created a tool called eslint-interactive which wraps ESLint. This tool groups all problems by rule and outputs formatted number of problems per rule. In addition to the breakdown of problems per rule, it also outputs the number of fixable problems and other hints to help developers fix problems."],"features":["Show a summary of all problems (called \"warnings\" or \"errors\" in ESLint) so that the whole picture can be easily understood","Showing the details of each problem will confuse developers.","Provide an efficient way to fix many problems","eslint --fix is one of the best ways to fix problems efficiently, but it auto-fixes all rule problems at once.","Depending on the rule, auto-fix may affect the behavior of the code, so auto-fix should be done with care."],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/mizdra/eslint-interactive/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"1ba3d30c1494f6bab40e27bc0cba53a31a3219ea4b01e50b54fd1ca26571fc21","extractedAt":"2026-10-11T09:01:45.386Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-11T09:01:45.386Z","projectInsights":{"version":1,"whatItDoes":{"text":"The default ESLint output contains a lot of useful messages for developers, such as the source of the error and hints for fixing it. While this works for many use cases, it does not work well in situations where many messages are reported. For example, when introducing ESLint into a project, or when making big changes to the eslint.config.js of a project. In these situations, the output of ESLint can be quite large, making it difficult for developers to analyze the output. It is also difficult for the developer to fix messages mechanically, because messages of many rules are mixed up in the…","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/mizdra/eslint-interactive/blob/main/README.md"},"audience":[{"text":"So, I created a tool called eslint-interactive which wraps ESLint. This tool groups all problems by rule and outputs formatted number of problems per rule. In addition to the breakdown of problems per rule, it also outputs the number of fixable problems and other hints to help developers fix problems.","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/mizdra/eslint-interactive/blob/main/README.md"}],"requirements":[],"fingerprint":"1ba3d30c1494f6bab40e27bc0cba53a31a3219ea4b01e50b54fd1ca26571fc21","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/mizdra/eslint-interactive/blob/main/README.md","checkedAt":"2026-10-11T09:01:45.387Z"},"overviewRepoUpdated":"2026-05-16T07:16:21Z","readmeSnapshot":{"version":1,"status":"saved","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/mizdra/eslint-interactive/blob/main/READ